1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,446
A shot put.

2
00:00:01,446 --> 00:00:02,493
A long jump.

3
00:00:02,493 --> 00:00:04,892
A fielder's throw from the boundary.

4
00:00:05,292 --> 00:00:09,512
In all three, the goal is the
same: land as far away as possible.

5
00:00:09,962 --> 00:00:14,667
On the college ground, Aarav throws
a ball at fourteen metres per second.

6
00:00:14,667 --> 00:00:17,748
Once at fifteen degrees,
once at seventy five.

7
00:00:18,098 --> 00:00:22,312
His puzzle: the flat throw or the
high throw, which one lands farther?

8
00:00:22,812 --> 00:00:24,217
Most people pick one.

9
00:00:24,217 --> 00:00:26,946
By the end, you will see why neither wins.

10
00:00:27,596 --> 00:00:29,665
So what is horizontal range?

11
00:00:30,015 --> 00:00:34,138
It is the horizontal distance a
projectile covers before it comes back

12
00:00:34,138 --> 00:00:35,577
to its launch level.

13
00:00:35,977 --> 00:00:40,645
Only the horizontal part of the
velocity, u cos theta, carries the ball

14
00:00:40,645 --> 00:00:41,545
forward.

15
00:00:41,368 --> 00:00:43,407
And gravity never changes it.

16
00:00:43,807 --> 00:00:48,438
So the range is simply that horizontal
speed, times the whole time in the

17
00:00:48,438 --> 00:00:49,338
air.

18
00:00:48,947 --> 00:00:50,693
A good throw needs both.

19
00:00:51,343 --> 00:00:55,892
Let's launch a ball at fourteen metres
per second, forty five degrees up.

20
00:00:56,242 --> 00:00:57,625
Watch the readout.

21
00:00:57,625 --> 00:01:02,054
The horizontal velocity stays at
nine point nine metres per second.

22
00:01:02,454 --> 00:01:05,273
So x grows by the same
amount every second.

23
00:01:05,273 --> 00:01:08,342
On the graph, x against
t is a straight line.

24
00:01:08,742 --> 00:01:13,084
The ball lands after two point zero
two seconds, twenty metres away.

25
00:01:13,084 --> 00:01:14,434
That is the range.

26
00:01:14,934 --> 00:01:16,408
Slow-motion replay.

27
00:01:16,408 --> 00:01:17,708
Freeze halfway.

28
00:01:18,108 --> 00:01:20,782
The red arrow g only bends the path down.

29
00:01:20,782 --> 00:01:24,473
The blue arrow, v x, keeps
the same length all the way.

30
00:01:24,973 --> 00:01:27,652
Now the ghost copies, at equal times.

31
00:01:27,652 --> 00:01:29,811
Every step across is the same.

32
00:01:29,811 --> 00:01:33,135
So range equals v x,
times the time of flight.

33
00:01:33,785 --> 00:01:36,011
Now the formula, step by step.

34
00:01:36,361 --> 00:01:37,261
Step one.

35
00:01:37,187 --> 00:01:40,833
The time of flight is the
time up plus the time down.

36
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:43,381
T equals two u sine theta, over g.

37
00:01:44,131 --> 00:01:45,104
Step two.

38
00:01:45,104 --> 00:01:48,153
Range is the horizontal speed times T.

39
00:01:48,153 --> 00:01:52,756
So R equals u cos theta,
times two u sine theta over g.

40
00:01:53,506 --> 00:01:54,514
Step three.

41
00:01:54,514 --> 00:01:58,589
Two sine theta cos theta is
the same as sine two theta.

42
00:01:59,339 --> 00:02:00,280
Step four.

43
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,742
So R equals u squared
sine two theta, over g.

44
00:02:03,742 --> 00:02:05,970
The units work out to metres.

45
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:07,808
Remember this.

46
00:02:07,808 --> 00:02:12,098
Range is the horizontal speed,
times the whole time in the air.

47
00:02:12,798 --> 00:02:14,291
Now, Aarav's puzzle.

48
00:02:14,291 --> 00:02:16,097
Same speed, many angles.

49
00:02:16,447 --> 00:02:20,001
Fifteen degrees: fast across,
but it lands too soon.

50
00:02:20,001 --> 00:02:20,912
Ten metres.

51
00:02:21,312 --> 00:02:26,229
Seventy five degrees: a long time in
the air, but it barely moves forward.

52
00:02:26,229 --> 00:02:27,440
Also ten metres!

53
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:28,740
Your turn.

54
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:31,549
Which angle in between goes the farthest?

55
00:02:31,549 --> 00:02:32,872
Pause and decide.

56
00:02:36,072 --> 00:02:37,560
Forty five degrees!

57
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:41,108
It shares the speed equally
between up and across.

58
00:02:41,108 --> 00:02:44,851
Sine two theta is one,
so R is u squared over g.

59
00:02:44,851 --> 00:02:46,005
Twenty metres.

60
00:02:46,655 --> 00:02:49,522
On the graph, range
follows sine two theta.

61
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:53,544
It peaks at forty five, and
falls the same way on both sides.

62
00:02:54,144 --> 00:02:55,311
Side by side.

63
00:02:55,311 --> 00:02:58,681
Twenty degrees and seventy
degrees, same speed.

64
00:02:59,081 --> 00:03:02,196
Twenty is faster across,
but shorter in the air.

65
00:03:02,196 --> 00:03:05,310
Seventy is slower across,
but longer in the air.

66
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:06,997
Now you try.

67
00:03:06,997 --> 00:03:08,780
Which one lands farther?

68
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,130
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:13,330 --> 00:03:14,524
The same spot!

70
00:03:14,524 --> 00:03:16,911
Sine forty equals sine one forty.

71
00:03:16,911 --> 00:03:20,723
Two angles that add up to
ninety always share a range.

72
00:03:21,373 --> 00:03:23,109
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:23,459 --> 00:03:24,599
Back to Aarav.

74
00:03:24,599 --> 00:03:28,584
He throws at fourteen metres
per second, fifteen degrees up.

75
00:03:28,584 --> 00:03:30,510
How far away does it land?

76
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:34,701
Use R equals u squared
sine two theta, over g.

77
00:03:35,451 --> 00:03:38,211
u squared is one hundred and ninety six.

78
00:03:38,211 --> 00:03:42,113
Two theta is thirty degrees,
and sine thirty is one half.

79
00:03:42,863 --> 00:03:47,047
So R is one ninety six times
one half, over nine point eight.

80
00:03:47,797 --> 00:03:49,769
That is exactly ten metres.

81
00:03:49,769 --> 00:03:53,401
And seventy five degrees
lands at the very same spot.

82
00:03:53,401 --> 00:03:54,833
Neither throw wins.

83
00:03:55,483 --> 00:03:56,948
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:57,298 --> 00:04:02,256
Many students multiply the horizontal
speed by the time to the top only.

85
00:04:02,606 --> 00:04:04,484
That gives just half the range.

86
00:04:04,484 --> 00:04:07,083
Use the whole time of flight, up and down.

87
00:04:07,733 --> 00:04:09,155
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:09,155 --> 00:04:13,918
As numericals on the range, the best
angle, and angles that share a range.

89
00:04:14,518 --> 00:04:15,693
Exam level now.

90
00:04:15,693 --> 00:04:20,472
A ball lands at the same spot for
two launch angles, at the same speed.

91
00:04:20,472 --> 00:04:24,007
The times of flight are two
seconds and three seconds.

92
00:04:24,007 --> 00:04:25,192
Take g as ten.

93
00:04:25,192 --> 00:04:26,367
Find the range.

94
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:27,592
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:32,992 --> 00:04:34,430
The answer is B.

96
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:35,623
Thirty metres.

97
00:04:36,123 --> 00:04:40,756
The same range means the two angles
are theta, and ninety minus theta.

98
00:04:41,256 --> 00:04:46,342
So the times are two u sine theta
over g, and two u cos theta over g.

99
00:04:46,842 --> 00:04:48,078
Multiply them.

100
00:04:48,078 --> 00:04:51,024
T one times T two is two R over g.

101
00:04:51,024 --> 00:04:53,831
So R is ten times six, over two.

102
00:04:53,831 --> 00:04:55,067
Thirty metres.

103
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:57,127
Here is your recap.

104
00:04:57,127 --> 00:04:58,628
Screenshot this card.

105
00:05:01,828 --> 00:05:04,665
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

106
00:05:05,065 --> 00:05:05,965
One.

107
00:05:05,601 --> 00:05:09,166
Why does forty five degrees
give the greatest range?

108
00:05:10,066 --> 00:05:10,966
Two.

109
00:05:10,609 --> 00:05:14,387
Which angle has the same
range as thirty five degrees?

110
00:05:15,287 --> 00:05:16,187
Three.

111
00:05:15,882 --> 00:05:19,668
If g were half as big, how
far would the same throw go?

112
00:05:20,768 --> 00:05:25,964
Next time: a mixer jar spins at a
steady speed, yet everything inside is

113
00:05:25,964 --> 00:05:27,028
accelerating.

114
00:05:27,028 --> 00:05:27,928
How?

115
00:05:27,593 --> 00:05:30,496
We find out in uniform circular motion.
